.md
This is why `vec4` and 4×4 matrices appear throughout graphics code. A direction uses a fourth component of 0, so translating a point does not translate its direction vector.

Now we can write our simplified perspective projection as a matrix `P`. The matrix produces **clip coordinates**; the division that creates perspective comes afterward:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
This is why `vec4` and 4×4 matrices appear throughout graphics code. A direction uses a fourth component of 0, so translating a point does not translate its direction vector.

Now we can write our simplified perspective projection as a matrix `P`. The matrix produces **clip coordinates**; the division that creates perspective comes afterward:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
P[a_, \[Theta]_, n_] := (*GB[*){{Cot[(*FB[*)((\[Theta])(*,*)/(*,*)(2))(*]FB*)](*|*),(*|*)0(*|*),(*|*)0(*|*),(*|*)0}(*||*),(*||*){0(*|*),(*|*)a Cot[(*FB[*)((\[Theta])(*,*)/(*,*)(2))(*]FB*)](*|*),(*|*)0(*|*),(*|*)0}(*||*),(*||*){0(*|*),(*|*)0(*|*),(*|*)-1(*|*),(*|*)-2 n}(*||*),(*||*){0(*|*),(*|*)0(*|*),(*|*)-1(*|*),(*|*)0}}(*||*)(*1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAo5gUSYZmp5S6pyflFiSX5RcFsQBHfxJKizAoAs04KOA==*)(*]GB*);

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Let's multiply it out symbolically:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Let's multiply it out symbolically:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
P[a,\[Theta],n]. (*GB[*){{x}(*||*),(*||*){y}(*||*),(*||*){z}(*||*),(*||*){1}}(*||*)(*1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAo5gUSYZmp5S6pyflFiSX5RcFsQBHfxJKizAoAs04KOA==*)(*]GB*) // Flatten

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
{x Cot[(*FB[*)((\[Theta])(*,*)/(*,*)(2))(*]FB*)],a y Cot[(*FB[*)((\[Theta])(*,*)/(*,*)(2))(*]FB*)],-2 n-z,-z}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
The fourth component is `-z`. Dividing the first **three** components by it gives normalized device coordinates, including depth. In a vertex shader we leave that division to the hardwired graphics pipeline, after clipping:

```glsl
void main() {
  gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.0);
}
```

Read this from right to left. `position` starts in the mesh's object space. `modelViewMatrix` takes it into camera space, and `projectionMatrix` takes it into clip space. The renderer supplies these matrices and updates them as the object or camera moves. Its actual projection can be perspective or orthographic; `P` above illustrates the perspective case.

Let's recreate the projection in WL. Change the field of view to see the apparent size change; change `n` to move the near plane and change the depth mapping. The point-cloud illustration colors depth of each point

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
The fourth component is `-z`. Dividing the first **three** components by it gives normalized device coordinates, including depth. In a vertex shader we leave that division to the hardwired graphics pipeline, after clipping:

```glsl
void main() {
  gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.0);
}
```

Read this from right to left. `position` starts in the mesh's object space. `modelViewMatrix` takes it into camera space, and `projectionMatrix` takes it into clip space. The renderer supplies these matrices and updates them as the object or camera moves. Its actual projection can be perspective or orthographic; `P` above illustrates the perspective case.

Let's recreate the projection in WL. Change the field of view to see the apparent size change; change `n` to move the near plane and change the depth mapping. The point-cloud illustration colors depth of each point

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Fade: true
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
exPoints = ExampleData[{"Geometry3D","UtahTeapot"}, "VertexData"];
exPoints = With[{m = Mean[exPoints]}, (# - m - {0,0,1.0})&/@ exPoints];

ClearAll[project];
project[params__][vec3List_List] := With[{vec4 = P[params].Transpose[{Join[#, {1.}]}] // Flatten},
  {vec4[[1;;2]]/vec4[[4]], (vec4[[3]]/vec4[[4]] + 1)/2}
]& /@ vec3List

depthColor[z_List] := List@@(Blend[{Red, Orange, Yellow, White}, #]) &/@ z;

Manipulate[Grid[{
  {"World",Graphics3D[{
    PointSize[0.01], Blue, Point[exPoints],
    Black, Sphere[{0,0,0}, 0.05],
    Line[{{0,0,0}, {0,Tan[\[Theta]/2],-1.0}}],
    Line[{{0,0,0}, {0,-Tan[\[Theta]/2],-1.0}}],
    Red, Opacity[0.5], 
    Cuboid[n {Tan[\[Theta]/2],Tan[\[Theta]/2],-1.001}, n {-Tan[\[Theta]/2],-Tan[\[Theta]/2],-1.0}]
  }, PlotRange->Full, ImageSize->300,
  "OrthographicCameraZoom"->1.9]},
  {"Screen", Graphics[With[{res = exPoints // project[1.0, \[Theta], n] // Transpose}, {
    PointSize[0.01], GraphicsComplex[res[[1]],
      Point[Range[Length[exPoints]]],
      VertexColors -> depthColor[res[[2]]]
    ]
  }], PlotRange->1, Axes->True, ImageSize->300, FrameTicks->{{},{}}, Frame->True, "Controls"->False]}
}//Transpose, Alignment->{Center, Center}]//Deploy, {\[Theta], 0.5, 2.0, 0.5}, {n, 0.1,0.5,0.1}, ContinuousAction->True]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["F8534a5d3-bb49-4cca-b6f6-67944847e3cf"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgUJW5gamySaphjrJiWZWOqaJCcn6iaZpZnpmplbmphYmJinGienAQCakBX9"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### Mesh modulation

Now we know where and who to intervene - change the object-space position before multiplying by the matrices. The rest of the camera pipeline can stay as it is.

Start with a constant offset along each vertex's unit normal:

$$
\\mathbf{p}' = \\mathbf{p} + A\\mathbf{n}.
$$

On a plane, this translates the whole surface. On a sphere, it changes the radius. On a curved plot, each point moves in a different direction. For the saddle $z=xy$, an upward unit normal is like $(-y,-x,1)/\\sqrt{1+x^2+y^2}$.

Let's mess with it in WL first. The blue surface below is the original saddle; the orange surface is its normal offset. Notice that this is not simply adding a constant to z

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Mesh modulation

Now we know where and who to intervene - change the object-space position before multiplying by the matrices. The rest of the camera pipeline can stay as it is.

Start with a constant offset along each vertex's unit normal:

$$
\\mathbf{p}' = \\mathbf{p} + A\\mathbf{n}.
$$

On a plane, this translates the whole surface. On a sphere, it changes the radius. On a curved plot, each point moves in a different direction. For the saddle $z=xy$, an upward unit normal is like $(-y,-x,1)/\\sqrt{1+x^2+y^2}$.

Let's mess with it in WL first. The blue surface below is the original saddle; the orange surface is its normal offset. Notice that this is not simply adding a constant to z

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Module[{base, normal, offset},
  base = {u, v, u v};
  normal = {-v, -u, 1}/Sqrt[1 + u^2 + v^2];
  offset = base + 0.35 normal;
  ParametricPlot3D[Evaluate[{base, offset}], {u, -1, 1}, {v, -1, 1},
    PlotStyle -> {Blue, Orange}, Mesh -> None,
    BoxRatios -> Automatic, ImageSize -> 420]
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["F0a8ebb33-b151-4e41-bd4a-e7e584f15083"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgUJGyRapCYlGRvrJhmaGuqapJoY6ialmCTqppqnmlqYpBmaGlgYAwCfkhXd"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
The GLSL version does the same vector addition for each existing vertex. In the definition below, the JavaScript wrapper loads 3D shared library and creates a `ShaderMaterial`. The strings inside `vertexShader` and `fragmentShader` as susual are GLSL programs executed on the GPU. These special constant vectors `position`, `normal`, and the camera matrices are supplied by the framework (Three.js) and a unique for each thread running on parallel. Note, that all running threads can't talk to each other and they only see their small chunk of memory exposed to a given vertex, normal, color and other custom buffer attributes - linear parallel arrays in GPU memory

!![1:]

In the contrast the fragment shader won't receive such slice, but it can get the interpolated per-pixel data generated by vertex shader (if needed).

:::help
See the [ShaderMaterial documentation](https://threejs.org/docs/pages/ShaderMaterial.html) for that interface.
:::


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
