%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
#### Fixing normals

For a flat base displaced to `z = h(x,y)`, the tangent vectors are `{1,0,D[h,x]}` and `{0,1,D[h,y]}`. Their cross product is `{-D[h,x],-D[h,y],1}`; normalize it to obtain the new normal.

In a 2D cross-section, the same construction gives `Normalize[{-h'(x),1}]`. The orange arrows below follow that normal, while the gray arrows keep pointing upward. Lighting with the gray arrows would make this entire wave behave as if it were flat. Notice how the corrected normals tilt on the slopes and return to vertical at crests and troughs:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
#### Fixing normals

For a flat base displaced to `z = h(x,y)`, the tangent vectors are `{1,0,D[h,x]}` and `{0,1,D[h,y]}`. Their cross product is `{-D[h,x],-D[h,y],1}`; normalize it to obtain the new normal.

In a 2D cross-section, the same construction gives `Normalize[{-h'(x),1}]`. The orange arrows below follow that normal, while the gray arrows keep pointing upward. Lighting with the gray arrows would make this entire wave behave as if it were flat. Notice how the corrected normals tilt on the slopes and return to vertical at crests and troughs:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Module[{height, slope},
  height[q_] := 0.3 Sin[3 q];
  slope[q_] := 0.9 Cos[3 q];
  Plot[height[x], {x, -2, 2},
    PlotRange -> {{-2.3, 2.3}, {-0.5, 0.8}},
    PlotStyle -> Blue, AspectRatio -> Automatic,
    Epilog -> Table[
      With[{p = {q, height[q]}},
        {Gray, Arrow[{p, p + {0, 0.3}}],
         Orange, Arrow[{p, p + 0.3 Normalize[{-slope[q], 1}]}]}
      ], {q, -1.8, 1.8, 0.3}]
  ]
]

