.md
### Ocean waves: put all pieces together

Our final material adds four waves with different directions, wavelengths, amplitudes, speeds and fixes normals! The displacement is still the same operation: `position + normal * h * uAmplitude`.

**Let's start with repairing the normals** We sample the wave field at the vertex and at three nearby points to estimate the gradient of the displacement height. Its normal component does not describe a slope along the surface, so we remove it:

$$
\\mathbf{g}_T=\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{n}(\\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{g}), \\qquad
\\mathbf{n}_{\\mathrm{wave}}\\approx
\\frac{\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{g}_T}{\\lVert\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{g}_T\\rVert}.
$$

For a planar base surface this gives the height-field normal, apart from finite-difference error if you wish. On a curved mesh it is a local approximation, small displacements relative to the surface's curvature will work best as expected.


#### Vertex shader
To nicely shade our waves, we generated the following `varying` attributes in addition in __our vertex shader__:

| Varying |  |
| --- | --- |
| `vNormalV` | The corrected normal, in camera space, for lighting |
| `vViewPos` | The displaced camera-space position, for the perspective view direction |
| `vObjPos` | The original object-space position, for procedural foam |
| `vHeight` | A normalized wave height, for color and crest selection |
| `vSlope` | The magnitude of the tangential gradient, to favor steep regions |

Then all of them are passed to fragment shader and interpolated linearly between 3 vertices automatically. 

#### Fragment shader

Troughs blend toward dark blue, crests toward cyan. The variable `depthMix` is a height-based color blend. A crest mask combines height and slope, and two scales of moving 3D value noise break that mask into patches. The noise interpolates pseudorandom values at the eight corners of a grid cell; so we do not use any texture image.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Ocean waves: put all pieces together

Our final material adds four waves with different directions, wavelengths, amplitudes, speeds and fixes normals! The displacement is still the same operation: `position + normal * h * uAmplitude`.

**Let's start with repairing the normals** We sample the wave field at the vertex and at three nearby points to estimate the gradient of the displacement height. Its normal component does not describe a slope along the surface, so we remove it:

$$
\\mathbf{g}_T=\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{n}(\\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{g}), \\qquad
\\mathbf{n}_{\\mathrm{wave}}\\approx
\\frac{\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{g}_T}{\\lVert\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{g}_T\\rVert}.
$$

For a planar base surface this gives the height-field normal, apart from finite-difference error if you wish. On a curved mesh it is a local approximation, small displacements relative to the surface's curvature will work best as expected.


#### Vertex shader
To nicely shade our waves, we generated the following `varying` attributes in addition in __our vertex shader__:

| Varying |  |
| --- | --- |
| `vNormalV` | The corrected normal, in camera space, for lighting |
| `vViewPos` | The displaced camera-space position, for the perspective view direction |
| `vObjPos` | The original object-space position, for procedural foam |
| `vHeight` | A normalized wave height, for color and crest selection |
| `vSlope` | The magnitude of the tangential gradient, to favor steep regions |

Then all of them are passed to fragment shader and interpolated linearly between 3 vertices automatically. 

#### Fragment shader

Troughs blend toward dark blue, crests toward cyan. The variable `depthMix` is a height-based color blend. A crest mask combines height and slope, and two scales of moving 3D value noise break that mask into patches. The noise interpolates pseudorandom values at the eight corners of a grid cell; so we do not use any texture image.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Fade: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.js
let THREE;
core.WaterMaterial = async (args, env) => {
  if (!THREE) {
    await interpretate.shared.THREE.load()
    THREE = interpretate.shared.THREE.THREE;
  }

  // Optional WL arguments: amplitude, frequency, speed.
  const values = await Promise.all(args.map(arg => interpretate(arg, env)));
  const [amplitude = 0.25, frequency = 4.0, speed = 1.0] = values;
  if (![amplitude, frequency, speed].every(Number.isFinite) || frequency <= 0) {
    throw new Error("WaterMaterial expects numeric amplitude/speed and positive frequency.");
  }

  return function() {
    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
      name: 'WaterMaterial',
      uniforms: {
        uTime:          { value: 0.0 },
        uAmplitude:     { value: amplitude },   
        uFrequency:     { value: frequency },   
        uSpeed:         { value: speed },
        uDeepColor:     { value: new THREE.Color(0x0a3d6b) },
        uShallowColor:  { value: new THREE.Color(0x2fa4d9) },
        uFoamColor:     { value: new THREE.Color(0xffffff) },
        uFoamThreshold: { value: 0.55 },   
        uOpacity:       { value: 0.92 }
      },
      vertexShader: `
        uniform float uTime, uAmplitude, uFrequency, uSpeed;
        varying vec3 vNormalV;
        varying vec3 vViewPos;
        varying vec3 vObjPos;
        varying float vHeight;   // normalized wave height, about -1..1
        varying float vSlope;

        // Sum of directional waves defined in 3D (object space),
        // Sample the same field on flat or curved meshes.
        float waves(vec3 p) {
          float t = uTime * uSpeed;
          float h = 0.0;
          h += 0.50 * sin(dot(p, normalize(vec3( 1.0, 0.3, 0.6))) * uFrequency        + t * 1.1);
          h += 0.30 * sin(dot(p, normalize(vec3(-0.7, 0.8, 0.2))) * uFrequency * 1.7  + t * 1.7);
          h += 0.15 * sin(dot(p, normalize(vec3( 0.2,-0.5, 1.0))) * uFrequency * 2.9  + t * 2.3);
          h += 0.08 * sin(dot(p, normalize(vec3( 0.9, 0.9,-0.4))) * uFrequency * 4.7  + t * 3.1);
          return h; // max amplitude sum = 1.03
        }

        void main() {
          vec3 n = normalize(normal);
          float h = waves(position);

          // 1. Estimate the object-space gradient of displacement height.
          float e = 0.01 / uFrequency;
          vec3 g = vec3(
            waves(position + vec3(e, 0.0, 0.0)) - h,
            waves(position + vec3(0.0, e, 0.0)) - h,
            waves(position + vec3(0.0, 0.0, e)) - h
          ) / e * uAmplitude;

          // 2. Project onto the tangent plane; approximate the new normal.
          vec3 tang = g - n * dot(g, n);
          vec3 nWave = normalize(n - tang);

          // 3. Displace, then apply the usual camera/projection transforms.
          vec3 displaced = position + n * h * uAmplitude;

          vec4 mv = modelViewMatrix * vec4(displaced, 1.0);
          vViewPos = mv.xyz;
          vNormalV = normalize(normalMatrix * nWave);
          vObjPos = position;
          vHeight = h / 1.03;
          vSlope = length(tang);

          gl_Position = projectionMatrix * mv;
        }
      `,
      fragmentShader: `
        uniform float uTime, uSpeed, uFoamThreshold, uOpacity;
        uniform vec3 uDeepColor, uShallowColor, uFoamColor;
        varying vec3 vNormalV;
        varying vec3 vViewPos;
        varying vec3 vObjPos;
        varying float vHeight;
        varying float vSlope;

        float hash(vec3 p) {
          p = fract(p * 0.3183099 + 0.1);
          p *= 17.0;
          return fract(p.x * p.y * p.z * (p.x + p.y + p.z));
        }
        float noise(vec3 x) {
          vec3 i = floor(x), f = fract(x);
          f = f * f * (3.0 - 2.0 * f);
          return mix(
            mix(mix(hash(i), hash(i + vec3(1,0,0)), f.x),
                mix(hash(i + vec3(0,1,0)), hash(i + vec3(1,1,0)), f.x), f.y),
            mix(mix(hash(i + vec3(0,0,1)), hash(i + vec3(1,0,1)), f.x),
                mix(hash(i + vec3(0,1,1)), hash(i + vec3(1,1,1)), f.x), f.y),
            f.z);
        }

        void main() {
          vec3 n = normalize(vNormalV);
          if (!gl_FrontFacing) n = -n;
          // Three.js supplies isOrthographic with the camera uniforms.
          vec3 v = isOrthographic ? vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0) : normalize(-vViewPos);
          vec3 l = normalize(vec3(1.0));

          // base water color: deeper in troughs, lighter on crests
          float depthMix = smoothstep(-1.0, 1.0, vHeight);
          vec3 col = mix(uDeepColor, uShallowColor, depthMix);

          // Camera-space diffuse, specular, and Fresnel-like lighting.
          float diff = clamp(dot(n, l), 0.0, 1.0) * 0.6 + 0.4;
          float spec = pow(max(dot(n, normalize(l + v)), 0.0), 90.0);
          float fres = pow(1.0 - max(dot(n, v), 0.0), 3.0);
          col = col * diff + uShallowColor * fres * 0.5 + vec3(spec) * 1.2;

          // foam: on crests
          
          float crest = smoothstep(uFoamThreshold, 1.0, vHeight + vSlope * 0.6);
          float nz = noise(vObjPos * 25.0 + vec3(0.0, uTime * uSpeed * 0.6, uTime * uSpeed * 0.3)) * 0.65
                   + noise(vObjPos * 60.0 - vec3(uTime * uSpeed * 0.4)) * 0.35;
          float foam = smoothstep(0.35, 0.75, nz * crest * 1.6 + crest * 0.25);
          col = mix(col, uFoamColor, clamp(foam, 0.0, 1.0));

          gl_FragColor = vec4(col, uOpacity);
        }
      `,
      lights: false,
      toneMapped: false,
      transparent: true,
      side: THREE.DoubleSide
    });

    const start = performance.now();
    material.onBeforeRender = () => {
      material.uniforms.uTime.value = (performance.now() - start) / 1000;
    };

    return material;
  }
}

